content.text:
Now there's a visible light pool at center with the player faintly visible (I can see a small silhouette in the pool at center). Slightly better but still hard to read. Hmm, the light pool center at ~514,350 and the player at center. The character is small (about 30px) and dark.

I think a rim light / silhouette highlight is needed. Let's draw a subtle warm outline around the player when it's dark: in drawPlayer, if nightFactor() > .35, before drawing body, draw a soft "aura" ellipse at feet? The easiest visual: draw a faint warm/cream contour around the player shape (torso/head) that survives darkness. Since the lighting overlay will darken the contour too, use very bright color like rgba(255,241,210,.9) — after .7 darkness it becomes ~.3 visible. But it's under the dark overlay so we don't need extra pass. Draw a 1.2px bright outline around the torso and head with low alpha.

Add in drawPlayer:
```js
const nightGlow = nightFactor() > .3;
...
if (nightGlow) {
  ctx.strokeStyle = 'rgba(255,236,190,.55)'; ctx.lineWidth = 1.1*z;
  // torso outline
  ctx.beginPath(); ... path of coat ... stroke
  // head arc
  ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(hx, hy, 4.6*z, 0, TAU); ctx.stroke();
}
```
But this is a bright "halo" which might look nice, like a lantern rim.

Alternatively add a shoulder lamp glow always at night? The game likely has flashlight; we can auto-enable flashlight at night on new game. That would make player and scene visible directionally. But flashlight cone only when on. In a survival game, the flashlight is an item? Let's check `flashlightOn` toggling and whether player has flashlight item. `G.player.flashlightOn` maybe always available. Search.

